GitOps and Kubernetes Relationship

✨ Hello DevOps Learners!
Continuing my GitOps learning journey after completing Ansible, today I’m exploring the relationship between GitOps and Kubernetes. 🚀
GitOps is widely adopted in modern DevOps environments because it works perfectly with Kubernetes’ declarative infrastructure model.

🔹 Why GitOps Works Well with Kubernetes
 Kubernetes uses a declarative configuration approach, where the desired state of the system is defined using YAML manifests.
GitOps follows the same principle:
Infrastructure and application configurations are stored in Git repositories
GitOps tools continuously ensure the cluster state matches what is defined in Git
This makes Kubernetes a natural platform for implementing GitOps workflows.

🔹 Kubernetes Controllers and GitOps
 Kubernetes already uses controllers to maintain system state.
 For example:
If a pod crashes, Kubernetes automatically recreates it.
GitOps tools extend this concept by ensuring:
The cluster configuration matches the Git repository
Any configuration drift is automatically corrected.

🔹 Benefits of Combining GitOps with Kubernetes
 Using GitOps with Kubernetes provides:
Automated and consistent deployments
Version-controlled infrastructure
Easy rollback using Git history
Improved visibility and collaboration

💡 Key Insight:
 GitOps and Kubernetes both rely on the declarative desired-state model, which makes them a powerful combination for modern cloud-native deployments.
